1. { -KEY- } Ba6 $1 { In the initial position, Black's main tactical weakness was obviously their vulnerable King. However, White did not have enough attackers. The plan was therefore to get the e1 Rook involved on the b-file and get the b5 Bishop out of the way. However, both of those plans fail to a common defensive idea - Black slipping away using the a7 square. } ( 1. Rb1 $4 a6 { White has no good discovered checks and Black's King cannot be caught as he would slip to either a7 or c7. } ) 
 ( 1. Ba4 $4 a6 { Once again, Black can save themselves by giving their King two escape routes - a7 and c7. } 2. Qd4 { White can control either a7 or c7, but not both at the same time. } Kc7 { Recognising that Black is barely saved by this one defensive resource, we can use reciprocal thinking to prevent Black from playing ...a6 altogether with an immediate 1. Ba6! } ) 
bxa6 { Other moves don't stop the attack. For example, } ( 1... Bd7 2. Rb1 Ka5 3. Rb5 Kxa6 ( 3... Bxb5 4. Qxb5 ) 
4. Qa4 Qa5 5. Qxa5 ) 
2. Rb1+ { Now that White's Rook can arrive on the b-file efficiently, Black has no time to create any escape routes and will get mated shortly. } Ka5 3. Qb4# 
