1... { -KEY- } Nh5 { White was just about to create a second Queen, but their King is in great danger. Hence, before rushing into any checks, we get rid of their g3 escape square ahead of time. The threat is now ...Rh1#, which cannot be stopped. } 2. Qc6+ ( 2. g3 { Any attempt to vacate the g2 square will weaken the second rank for checkmate. } Re2 ) 
Kh7 { There is no need to calculate all the moves to realise that this is the only logical way forward. There is no sense in running our King outside and getting in trouble. } ( 2... Kg5 3. h4 Kxh4 4. g3 Nxg3 5. Qc4 Ne4 6. Qc2 Rh1 7. Kg2 { Black can have a perpetual check on the first rank at best, but there is no checkmate. } ) 
 ( 2... Kf7 3. Qf3 { Forking the King and Knight. } Kg6 4. Qg4 Kh7 5. Qxh5 { The Knight is gone and White's King is safe. } ) 
 ( 2... Kf5 3. g4 Kf4 4. Qg2 { Again, Black has no checkmate, and instead has to deal with the hanging h5 Knight and advanced connected passed a- and b-pawns. } ) 
3. Qe4+ { The only way to stop mate is to give up the Queen. } Rxe4 4. dxe4 Rxb7 { Easy victory from here. } 
