1... { -KEY- } Nd4 { In the initial position, Black had two tactical weaknesses - The hanging f5 Knight, and the unprotected f6 pawn. f6 could also be considered a critical square since it is near Black's King. White, on the other hand, had a hanging c4 Bishop and an unprotected g4 pawn. g4 was likewise considered a critical square because if Black's Queen gets access to g4, White's King would be in trouble. After all of the above is identified, we can start thinking about candidate moves. Moving our Knight away from f5 would automatically save the Knight while attacking g4. Hence, the final task is to move our Knight to a square that protects f6, and ...Nd4 is the only move. } ( 1... Nh4 $4 { Other Knight moves will leave f6 hanging. } 2. Nxf6 ) 
2. Ba2 { If White saves the Bishop, White gets mated. } ( 2. Re4 { If White protects g4, White's Bishop falls. } bxc4 ) 
 ( 2. Re7 { Since our d4 Knight controls the critical e2 square, this move is not concerning. } Qxe7 { Removing the defender of e2. } 3. Nxe7 Ne2 { We have sacrificed our Queen for a Rook, but we are getting the Queen back now. } 4. Kf2 Nxc3 5. bxc3 bxc4 { At the end of the day, we are up a clear Rook. } ) 
 ( 2. Re7 Qxe7 3. Nxe7 Ne2 { Black gets back the queen with interest. } ) 
Qxg4+ 3. Kf2 Qf3+ { Basic checkmating pattern from here. } 4. Kg1 Qg3+ 5. Kh1 Rh8# 
