1... { -KEY- } Qa3 { Our passed b-pawn is our win condition. Therefore, the only potential ideas are to bring our Queen to c1 or a1. This calls for the Superposition Technique. As we calculate both lines simultaneously, we must recognise our key tactical weakness, bearing in mind that White will try to exploit that. Here, our only weakness is our King, which is exposed along the seventh and eighth ranks. This means White will try for a perpetual check. } ( 1... Qc3 $4 2. Qe4 { We can safely assume that White will try to go after our King. } Qc1 3. Kg2 b1=Q $11 4. Qe7 { It's a draw by perpetual check. If our Queen were on a1 instead of c1, we could block with ...Qg7 and win the game with our extra Queen. The Superposition is now clarified, and we can confirm that 1...Qa3 is the correct way to start. } ) 
2. Qe4 Qa1+ 3. Kg2 b1=Q 4. Qe7+ Qg7 { Defending the King. Black will win with the extra Queen soon enough. } 
