1... { -KEY- } e4 { e4 is obviously a critical square since Black almost has a successful pawn fork on that square. A significantly more subtle critical square is d4. Black has one attacker, the c6 Knight, threatening a fork on that square. On the other hand, White only has one defender, the e3 pawn. The f3 Knight is not a real defender because it is pinned. This information should get us looking for ways to add an attacker to e4 or somehow eliminate the e3 pawn as a defender of the d4 square. This is when opening up the e-file should come to mind. If Black's e8 Rook is unleashed, White's e3 pawn would be pinned, and Black would have the ...Nd4+ fork. Hence, 1...e4 stands out as a good first candidate move. } 2. Bxe4 { Once we mentally arrive at this position in our calculation, we must reassess the game. Now, the e-file is half-open while White's unprotected e4 Bishop is standing in the way. So, we need only add an attacker to get rid of it. At the same time, we must be mindful to not allow the Bishop to escape the e-file with tempo. Otherwise, White will have time to defend against the Knight fork. } Bf5 $1 ( 2... Qg4 $4 { This allows White to move the Bishop with tempo. } 3. Bh7 Kh8 4. Kf1 { White has time to save themselves from the fork. } ) 
 ( 2... f5 $4 { White can similarly move the Bishop with check, ensuring that Black has no time to fork White's royal pieces. } 3. Bd5 Kh8 4. Kf1 { Once again, this extra tempo given to White proves critical. } ) 
3. Bxf5 { If the Bishop moves, then comes the fork. } Nd4+ 4. Kf1 Nxc2 { White has a favourable material imbalance of a Queen vs one minor piece + two pawns. } 
