[FEN "7Q/1pqk2r1/2p4p/4rpp1/8/7P/1P3P1K/2R1R3 w - - 0 1"]

1. { -KEY- } f4 { Blocking the b8-h2 diagonal while attacking e5 Rook. Black cannot save both Rooks at once. In the initial position, Black was at great risk. Their g7 Rook was hanging and their King was very exposed on the open central files, with nowhere to run. However, White could not exploit either of these weaknesses right away because of Black's discovered check against our King. } ( 1. Qxg7 $4 Re7 { Counter check! White will now lose the Queen. Therefore, using reciprocal thinking, we realise we ought to deal with this discovered check first. Ideally, our move should come with tempo, so that Black has no time to save the g7 Rook. Incidentally, 1.f4 checks all the boxes. } ) 
 ( 1. Rcd1 $4 Ke6 { Black can consolidate the position by connecting the c7 Queen and g7 Rook. } ) 
Rxe1 ( 1... Ree7 { Black saves both Rooks in this line, but now has no shield along the open central files. } 2. Rcd1 { Black has to give up the Queen now. } Qd6 3. Rxd6 Kxd6 4. Qxh6 { The game is over for Black. } ) 
2. Qxg7+ { Regaining the Rook with check. } Re7 { Black's best try is to block the check and save the Rook. } 3. Rd1+ { The weak Black King turns out to be problematic. Black will either get mated or lose the Queen. } Qd6 ( 3... Ke8 4. Qg8 ) 
 ( 3... Ke6 4. Qg6 ) 
4. Rxd6+ 
